The biggest practical problem with status codes isn't choosing them — it's keeping them consistent as the codebase grows. Without a centralized strategy, different developers return different codes for the same situation. One controller throws `ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)`, another returns `ResponseEntity.status(404)`, and a third returns null and accidentally produces 200. Clients can't predict what to expect, and monitoring dashboards produce misleading alerts.

**The fix is a domain exception hierarchy mapped to status codes in a single `@RestControllerAdvice`.** Each business exception carries its implicit status — `ResourceNotFoundException` is always 404, `ConflictException` is always 409, `ValidationException` is always 422. Controllers throw them freely; the advice class maps them consistently:

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
        // 422 for semantic failures, not 400 — syntax is valid, content is wrong
        ProblemDetail detail = ProblemDetail.forStatus(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY);
        detail.setProperty("errors", ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors()
            .stream().map(e -> e.getField() + ": " + e.getDefaultMessage()).toList());
        return detail;
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleUnexpected(Exception ex) {
        log.error("Unhandled exception", ex); // log full trace, never expose it
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
            "An unexpected error occurred");
    }
}
```

Using Spring 6's `ProblemDetail` returns a standardized RFC 7807 body — `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` — that client teams can depend on across every endpoint. The catch-all at the bottom always returns 500 and **never includes the exception message or stack trace** in the response. Leaking internal exception details in a 500 is one of the most common API security mistakes — attackers use error messages to probe internal structure. Log everything server-side; return nothing client-side.

Choosing a versioning strategy is only half the problem. **How you retire old versions is where most teams fail.** Without a clear deprecation timeline, clients never migrate — they stay on v1 forever because "it still works," and you're maintaining two code paths indefinitely.

The IETF `Sunset` header (RFC 8594) is the standard mechanism for communicating deprecation dates. Adding `Sunset: Sat, 1 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT` to every v1 response tells clients — and monitoring tools — exactly when the version goes away. Pair it with `Deprecation: true` and a `Link` header pointing to the migration guide:

```java
@GetMapping("/api/v1/users/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<UserV1Response> getUserV1(@PathVariable Long id,
        HttpServletResponse response) {
    // Deprecation headers on every v1 response — monitoring alerts on these
    response.setHeader("Deprecation", "true");
    response.setHeader("Sunset", "Sat, 01 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT");
    response.setHeader("Link", "</api/v2/users/{id}>; rel=\"successor-version\"");
    return ResponseEntity.ok(userService.findByIdV1(id));
}
```

**Stripe's version pinning approach is the most client-friendly model.** Each API key is created with a version — whatever version was current at creation time. New API behavior ships as additive changes to all versions. Only breaking changes require a new version, and clients are pinned to their creation-date version forever unless they explicitly opt in to the new version. This eliminates forced migrations and gives clients a safe, predictable environment. The tradeoff is infrastructure complexity — the server must maintain backward-compatible behavior across many versions simultaneously.

For semantic versioning vs date-based: **semver (v1, v2, v3) is clearer for public APIs** where you want to signal the magnitude of changes. Date-based versioning (`2024-01-15`) is better for APIs that change frequently and want to provide granular rollback points without major version theatre.

The most commonly missed detail in error handling design isn't the status code — it's **the response body structure.** Every team invents their own error schema: some use `{"error": "..."}`, some use `{"message": "...", "code": 42}`, some use `{"success": false, "reason": "..."}`. Clients that consume multiple services write custom parsing for each. Spring 6 solves this with built-in support for RFC 7807 `ProblemDetail`.

`ProblemDetail` defines five standard fields: `type` (a URI identifying the problem category), `title` (human-readable summary), `status` (HTTP status code), `detail` (specific description), and `instance` (URI of the specific occurrence). Any additional fields — like a list of validation errors — go in extension properties.

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail
            .forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());
        problem.setType(URI.create("https://api.example.com/errors/not-found"));
        problem.setInstance(URI.create(request.getRequestURI()));
        return problem;
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail.forStatus(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY);
        // Extension property — list field violations for client display
        problem.setProperty("violations",
            ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
                .map(e -> Map.of("field", e.getField(), "message", e.getDefaultMessage()))
                .toList());
        return problem;
    }
}
```

**`@RestControllerAdvice` ensures `ProblemDetail` is serialized to JSON** without an explicit `@ResponseBody` on each handler method. The `violations` extension property gives frontend teams the field-level errors they need to display inline next to form fields — without parsing free-text messages. For Spring Boot 3.x, enabling `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true` also makes the default Spring MVC exception handlers (like `NoResourceFoundException`) automatically return `ProblemDetail` instead of the legacy Whitelabel Error Page.

**One additional Spring Boot 3.x detail:** setting `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true` in `application.properties` makes all of Spring MVC's built-in exception handlers — like `NoResourceFoundException` and `HttpRequestMethodNotSupportedException` — automatically return `ProblemDetail` responses instead of the Whitelabel HTML error page. This gives you RFC 7807-compliant error bodies for framework exceptions without writing a handler for each one.

Built-in annotations like `@NotNull`, `@Size`, `@Email` handle most cases. But business rules — "phone number must match the country code" or "end date must be after start date" — need custom validators.

A custom constraint has two parts: the annotation and the validator.

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Constraint(validatedBy = DateRangeValidator.class)
public @interface ValidDateRange {
    String message() default "End date must be after start date";
    Class<?>[] groups() default {};
    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};
}

public class DateRangeValidator implements ConstraintValidator<ValidDateRange, EventRequest> {
    public boolean isValid(EventRequest req, ConstraintValidatorContext ctx) {
        if (req.getStartDate() == null || req.getEndDate() == null) return true;
        return req.getEndDate().isAfter(req.getStartDate());
    }
}
```

**The `@Target(ElementType.TYPE)` is key for cross-field validation** — it goes on the class, not a field, giving the validator access to all fields. Field-level validators only see their own field's value. Returning `true` for null values is a convention — let `@NotNull` handle null checks separately.

For more complex validation messages, `ConstraintValidatorContext` lets you disable the default message and add custom field-level errors programmatically. This is useful when a single class-level validator needs to report which specific field caused the failure — without this, the error message appears on the class rather than a field, making it harder for clients to display.

Standard `@Valid` applies all constraints on a class simultaneously — every annotation fires on every request. This causes a problem with update operations. A `User` DTO has an `id` field that **must** be absent on creation (the server assigns it) and **must** be present on update (to identify which record to change). With a single DTO and `@Valid`, you can't express this rule without splitting into two separate DTO classes.

**Validation groups solve this.** You define marker interfaces to represent the context, then assign constraints to groups. Spring's `@Validated` (not Jakarta's `@Valid`) accepts a group class and activates only the constraints belonging to that group:

```java
// Marker interfaces — no methods, just type identity
public interface OnCreate {}
public interface OnUpdate {}

@Data
public class UserRequest {
    @Null(groups = OnCreate.class)   // id must be absent on create
    @NotNull(groups = OnUpdate.class) // id must be present on update
    private Long id;

    @NotBlank(groups = {OnCreate.class, OnUpdate.class})
    private String name;

    @Email(groups = OnCreate.class) // email only validated on create
    private String email;
}

@PostMapping("/users")
public ResponseEntity<UserResponse> create(
        @Validated(OnCreate.class) @RequestBody UserRequest req) { ... }

@PutMapping("/users/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<UserResponse> update(
        @Validated(OnUpdate.class) @RequestBody UserRequest req) { ... }
```

The `Default` group is the standard group that applies when no group is specified — constraints without a `groups` attribute belong to `Default`. When you switch to explicit groups, **constraints without a group attribute no longer fire** unless you include `Default.class` in your `@Validated` call. This surprises developers who add groups to some constraints and expect un-grouped constraints to still run.

For complex validation order (validate group A, then group B only if A passes), use `@GroupSequence`. This is valuable for expensive validations — skip the database uniqueness check if the basic format validation already failed.

`HandlerInterceptor` has three methods. Knowing which one to use for which concern is the practical skill interviewers test when they ask about interceptors.

`preHandle(request, response, handler)` runs before the controller. The `handler` parameter is a `HandlerMethod` object — you can inspect the controller method's annotations at runtime. Return `false` to abort the request; the response must already be written before returning false. This is where you check for method-level security annotations, enforce rate limits per endpoint, or record the start time for latency measurement.

```java
@Component
public class LatencyInterceptor implements HandlerInterceptor {
    private static final String START_ATTR = "requestStart";

    @Override
    public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, Object handler) {
        req.setAttribute(START_ATTR, System.currentTimeMillis()); // store start time on request
        return true; // continue processing
    }

    @Override
    public void afterCompletion(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res,
            Object handler, Exception ex) {
        long start = (Long) req.getAttribute(START_ATTR);
        long latency = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
        // Tag with handler name for per-endpoint metrics
        metrics.recordLatency(handler.toString(), latency, res.getStatus());
    }
}
```

`postHandle(request, response, handler, modelAndView)` runs after the controller but before view rendering. It's only useful for MVC applications with views — in REST APIs where there's no `ModelAndView`, this hook is empty. `afterCompletion` is the most important hook for REST APIs — it runs after the response is committed, even if an exception occurred, making it the right place for cleanup, metrics, and closing spans.

Register interceptors in a `WebMvcConfigurer`:

```java
@Configuration
public class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {
    @Override
    public void addInterceptors(InterceptorRegistry registry) {
        registry.addInterceptor(latencyInterceptor)
            .addPathPatterns("/api/**")
            .excludePathPatterns("/api/health");
    }
}
```

The `addPathPatterns` call limits the interceptor to matched paths — unlike Filters, which receive every request. This is another advantage of interceptors for API-specific concerns: health checks and actuator endpoints are excluded without additional configuration.

Spring Data JPA doesn't have built-in cursor pagination, but the pattern is straightforward. The core idea is that the cursor encodes the sort field values of the last item returned. The next page query uses those values in a WHERE clause instead of OFFSET, letting the database use an index scan directly. Encode the cursor as a Base64-encoded JSON object containing the sort fields.

```java
@GetMapping("/orders")
public Page<OrderDto> getOrders(
        @RequestParam(required = false) String after,
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") int size) {
    Cursor cursor = after != null ? Cursor.decode(after) : null;
    // WHERE id > cursor.id ORDER BY id ASC LIMIT size+1
    List<Order> results = orderRepository.findAfterCursor(
        cursor != null ? cursor.getId() : 0L, PageRequest.of(0, size + 1));
    boolean hasNext = results.size() > size;
    if (hasNext) results = results.subList(0, size);
    String nextCursor = hasNext ? Cursor.encode(results.get(results.size()-1)) : null;
    return new CursorPage<>(results.stream().map(this::toDto).toList(), nextCursor);
}
```

The response includes the `nextCursor` only when more data exists, signaling the client to request the next page. **The `size + 1` trick is key — fetching one extra row tells you whether there's a next page without a separate `COUNT(*)` query, which is expensive on large tables. **The cursor should be opaque to the client** — Base64 encoding prevents clients from manipulating sort values directly.

**Annotation-level** with `@CrossOrigin(origins = "https://app.example.com")` applies to a single controller or method. Use it when only specific endpoints need cross-origin access. But it only works for endpoints handled by Spring MVC — it doesn't cover error responses or endpoints outside the MVC pipeline.

**Global MVC-level** via `WebMvcConfigurer` applies to all matched handler methods. This is the cleanest approach for most applications.

```java
@Configuration
public class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {
    @Override
    public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {
        registry.addMapping("/api/**")
            .allowedOrigins("https://app.example.com")
            .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE")
            .allowCredentials(true)
            .maxAge(3600);
    }
}
```

**`maxAge(3600)` tells browsers to cache the preflight response** for one hour, reducing OPTIONS requests from every API call to one per hour per endpoint. Without this, every non-simple request triggers two HTTP calls.

**Filter-level** with `CorsFilter` runs before DispatcherServlet — use it only when you need CORS on non-MVC endpoints (like actuator or static resources). Never combine filter-level with MVC-level for the same paths.

**Understanding why preflight requests exist:** the browser sends a preflight OPTIONS request before any non-simple cross-origin request (PUT, DELETE, requests with custom headers like `Authorization`, requests with `Content-Type: application/json`). The preflight asks the server: "will you accept this kind of request from this origin?" If the server doesn't respond correctly to OPTIONS — either because CORS isn't configured for that path, or because Spring Security intercepts it before the CORS handler runs — the browser cancels the actual request without ever sending it. This is why CORS errors are invisible in server logs: the request your code cares about never arrives. You only see the failed OPTIONS request, which is easy to miss when filtering logs by your endpoint path.

Testing CORS in isolation with `curl -X OPTIONS` is straightforward, but remember that `curl` doesn't enforce the same-origin policy — it will happily show you a response that the browser would reject. Always test CORS behavior in a real browser or use the Fetch API in the browser console to verify the actual browser experience.

The most common CORS bug in Spring Boot applications with Spring Security is: **CORS works in development, breaks in production when you add Spring Security.** The root cause is filter chain ordering.

Spring Security's `FilterChainProxy` runs before DispatcherServlet. It intercepts every request — including preflight OPTIONS requests. If Spring Security rejects the OPTIONS request before DispatcherServlet's CORS handling runs, the browser gets a 403 and the actual request never fires. Your `WebMvcConfigurer` CORS config is irrelevant because the request never reaches MVC.

The fix has two parts. First, call `.cors(Customizer.withDefaults())` in your Security configuration — this tells Spring Security to **delegate CORS decisions** to the `CorsConfigurationSource` bean (or the MVC `CorsRegistry` you've already configured). Second, ensure the security chain permits OPTIONS requests explicitly:

```java
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        // Delegate to MVC's CorsRegistry — do NOT configure CORS twice
        .cors(Customizer.withDefaults())
        .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable()) // stateless JWT API
        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
            // OPTIONS must pass — it carries no auth credentials
            .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.OPTIONS, "/**").permitAll()
            .anyRequest().authenticated())
        .build();
}

@Bean
public CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
    CorsConfiguration config = new CorsConfiguration();
    config.setAllowedOrigins(List.of("https://app.example.com"));
    config.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS"));
    config.setAllowedHeaders(List.of("Authorization", "Content-Type"));
    config.setAllowCredentials(true);
    config.setMaxAge(3600L); // cache preflight 1 hour — halves OPTIONS traffic
    UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource source = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
    source.registerCorsConfiguration("/api/**", config);
    return source;
}
```

**Never use `allowedOrigins("*")` with `allowCredentials(true)`** — the browser rejects this combination. The CORS spec prohibits wildcards when credentials are sent. Use explicit origins or switch to `allowedOriginPatterns("https://*.example.com")` which supports subdomain wildcards while remaining specific enough to be safe. The `allowedOriginPatterns` method was added specifically to handle this restriction without forcing developers to enumerate every subdomain.

The rule is simple: **CSRF protection is required when the browser sends credentials automatically.** Cookies are the primary case — the browser attaches them to every request matching the cookie's domain, without any action from the page. A malicious site can make a form POST to `bank.com/transfer` and the browser automatically includes the session cookie. Without CSRF protection, the bank processes it as legitimate.

JWT tokens in the `Authorization` header break this attack vector — the header is never added automatically, so cross-site requests arrive without credentials and get rejected by authentication. This is why stateless JWT APIs disable CSRF by default.

**The modern alternative to CSRF tokens is the `SameSite` cookie attribute.** Setting `SameSite=Lax` tells the browser not to send the cookie on cross-site sub-resource requests (AJAX, form POSTs from other domains), but to allow it on top-level navigation (clicking a link). `SameSite=Strict` blocks even navigation requests. Either setting prevents the CSRF attack without any server-side token validation:

```java
@Bean
public CookieSerializer cookieSerializer() {
    DefaultCookieSerializer serializer = new DefaultCookieSerializer();
    serializer.setSameSite("Lax");   // Prevents CSRF on AJAX/forms
    serializer.setUseSecureCookie(true); // HTTPS only — never omit this
    serializer.setHttpOnly(true);    // No JS access — blocks XSS session theft
    return serializer;
}
```

For **hybrid applications** — a Spring Boot backend that serves both a REST API (consumed by a React SPA) and server-side rendered pages — you often need CSRF for the form-based flows but want to disable it for the API endpoints. Spring Security supports this with separate security filter chains. Apply `csrf().disable()` to the `/api/**` chain and keep CSRF enabled on the web chain. The SameSite cookie approach eliminates this complexity by making CSRF protection a browser-level concern rather than a server-side token dance.

Auto-generated specs from controller method signatures are a starting point, not a finished product. Without annotations, response types for error cases are missing, parameter descriptions are generic, and the spec doesn't document authentication requirements.

**`@Operation`** adds summary and description to an endpoint. **`@ApiResponse`** documents specific response codes and their schemas — especially important for error responses that aren't in the method signature. **`@Parameter`** describes path variables and query parameters.

```java
@Operation(summary = "Get user by ID", description = "Returns a single user")
@ApiResponse(responseCode = "200", description = "User found")
@ApiResponse(responseCode = "404", description = "User not found",
    content = @Content(schema = @Schema(implementation = ProblemDetail.class)))
@GetMapping("/users/{id}")
public UserDto getUser(@Parameter(description = "User ID") @PathVariable Long id) { }
```

For authentication, **`@SecurityScheme`** at the class level documents the auth mechanism, and **`@SecurityRequirement`** on endpoints marks which ones need it. Without these, the Swagger UI has no way to send authenticated requests during testing.

An API documentation page that can't send authenticated requests is only half useful. Without security configuration, every call from the Swagger UI fails with 401 — developers can see the endpoint structure but can't test anything that requires authentication. Configuring JWT Bearer authentication in the spec unlocks the full testing experience.

The configuration declares a security scheme at the application level and applies it to specific endpoints. `@SecurityScheme` defines how authentication works; `@SecurityRequirement` marks which endpoints need it:

```java
@Configuration
@SecurityScheme(
    name = "bearerAuth",
    type = SecuritySchemeType.HTTP,
    scheme = "bearer",
    bearerFormat = "JWT"
)
public class OpenApiConfig {

    @Bean
    public OpenAPI openAPI() {
        return new OpenAPI()
            .info(new Info()
                .title("Order API")
                .version("1.0.0"))
            // Apply JWT auth globally to all endpoints
            .addSecurityItem(new SecurityRequirement().addList("bearerAuth"));
    }
}
```

With this config, the Swagger UI shows an **Authorize** button. Developers paste a JWT token once, and all subsequent requests include `Authorization: Bearer <token>` automatically.

**Disabling Swagger UI in production** is a security best practice. The spec itself isn't sensitive — it documents your public API. But a live Swagger UI in production gives attackers a ready-made testing interface. Disable it with an environment-specific property:

```yaml
# application-prod.yml
springdoc:
  swagger-ui:
    enabled: false
  api-docs:
    enabled: false  # Disable raw JSON spec too if truly internal
```

For **contract-first development**, export the spec from `/v3/api-docs` and use OpenAPI Generator to create client SDKs in TypeScript, Python, or any other language. The generated client is always in sync with your actual API — no manual maintenance. This is particularly valuable for public APIs where external teams consume your API: shipping the spec alongside a breaking change gives clients a machine-readable diff of what changed.

The response format selection happens in `AbstractMessageConverterMethodProcessor.writeWithMessageConverters()`. Understanding the three steps explains every content negotiation surprise you'll encounter.

**Step one: resolve the requested media type.** By default, Spring reads the `Accept` header. Spring also supports two alternative strategies — a query parameter (`?format=json`) and URL path extension (`.json`/`.xml`). Path extension was deprecated in Spring 5.3 and removed in Spring 6 due to security risks with path traversal. In practice, the `Accept` header is the only strategy you should rely on.

**Step two: find a matching converter.** Spring iterates through all registered `HttpMessageConverter` instances and calls `canWrite(returnType, mediaType)` on each. The first converter that returns `true` handles serialization. This is where the surprise happens — if `MappingJackson2XmlHttpMessageConverter` is registered (from the XML dataformat dependency), it answers `true` for `application/xml`.

To see this concretely, consider a minimal endpoint that works perfectly until someone adds the XML module.

```java
@GetMapping("/users/{id}")
public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return userService.findById(id);
}
```

With only Jackson core on the classpath, this always returns JSON. Add `jackson-dataformat-xml` to your POM, and a request with `Accept: application/xml` suddenly returns XML — without touching a single line of controller code.

**Step three: serialize and write.** The winning converter transforms the object and writes it to the `HttpServletResponse`. If no converter matches the requested type, Spring returns **406 Not Acceptable**.

The fix is explicit: declare what each endpoint supports using the `produces` attribute.

```java
@GetMapping(value = "/users/{id}", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return userService.findById(id);
}
```

With `produces` set, Spring rejects any request whose `Accept` header doesn't match — returning 406 instead of silently switching formats. This is the difference between an API that behaves predictably and one that surprises clients after a dependency upgrade.

You can also lock format globally through `ContentNegotiationConfigurer` in a `WebMvcConfigurer`. Setting `defaultContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)` ensures JSON is the fallback when the `Accept` header is missing or `*/*`, and `favorParameter(false)` disables the query parameter strategy entirely. Global configuration is cleaner than annotating every endpoint, but `produces` on individual endpoints gives you per-route control — useful when specific endpoints genuinely need to support XML (public APIs, government integrations, or legacy system interop).

The practical rule: **lock by default, open selectively.** Most internal APIs should lock to JSON via `produces` or global config. Only expose multiple formats on endpoints where external contracts require it — and document which formats are supported, because clients will discover them whether you intended it or not.

**Pattern 1: 201 Created with Location header.** The REST convention for POST endpoints that create resources. `ResponseEntity.created()` sets both the 201 status and the Location header pointing to the new resource.

```java
@PostMapping("/users")
public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(@RequestBody @Valid CreateUserRequest req) {
    User saved = userService.create(req);
    URI location = ServletUriComponentsBuilder.fromCurrentRequest()
            .path("/{id}").buildAndExpand(saved.getId()).toUri();
    return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(saved);
}
```

The `Location` header matters because API clients (and tools like Postman) use it to navigate to the newly created resource. Omitting it is a common shortcut that breaks client-side workflows.

**Pattern 2: Conditional 200/404.** When a resource might not exist and you want to return 404 without throwing an exception — useful when you consider "not found" a normal flow rather than an exceptional condition.

```java
@GetMapping("/users/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<User> getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return userService.findById(id)
            .map(ResponseEntity::ok)
            .orElse(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());
}
```

This is cleaner than throwing `ResponseStatusException` when the absence is expected. For true error scenarios (invalid data, authorization failures), let `@ControllerAdvice` handle the exception — don't manually build error `ResponseEntity` objects in every controller.

**Pattern 3: 204 No Content for DELETE.** Returning `ResponseEntity.noContent().build()` after a successful delete is semantically correct — the resource is gone, there's nothing to return. The alternative `@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)` on a void method achieves the same result with less code, so this is a matter of team convention.

The underlying principle: **use `@ResponseStatus` for fixed outcomes and `ResponseEntity` for dynamic ones.** Mixing both in the same method is confusing — pick one pattern and apply it consistently.

A production upload endpoint needs three things beyond the basic `MultipartFile` parameter: size limits, content type validation, and external storage.

```java
@PostMapping("/documents")
public ResponseEntity<DocumentResponse> upload(
        @RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file) {
    if (file.isEmpty()) throw new BadRequestException("File is empty");
    if (file.getSize() > 10_000_000) throw new BadRequestException("File exceeds 10MB");

    String contentType = file.getContentType();
    if (!ALLOWED_TYPES.contains(contentType))
        throw new BadRequestException("Unsupported file type: " + contentType);

    DocumentResponse saved = storageService.store(file);
    URI location = ServletUriComponentsBuilder.fromCurrentRequest()
            .path("/{id}").buildAndExpand(saved.getId()).toUri();
    return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(saved);
}
```

The `storageService` should write to **external storage** (S3, Azure Blob, MinIO) — not the local filesystem. Local storage breaks the moment you scale to multiple instances behind a load balancer, because the file exists on one node but subsequent requests may hit a different one. Even for single-instance deployments, a container restart wipes local storage.

The application properties guard the outer boundary: `spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size=10MB` and `spring.servlet.multipart.max-request-size=10MB`. These trigger a `MaxUploadSizeExceededException` before the request reaches your controller, which you handle in `@ControllerAdvice` to return a clean 413 Payload Too Large.

The naive download approach loads the file into a `byte[]` and returns it. This works until the file is large enough to exhaust heap memory. The production approach streams the file directly to the client.

```java
@GetMapping("/documents/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<Resource> download(@PathVariable Long id) {
    DocumentMeta meta = storageService.getMeta(id);
    InputStream stream = storageService.getStream(id);

    return ResponseEntity.ok()
            .contentType(MediaType.parseMediaType(meta.getContentType()))
            .header(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_DISPOSITION,
                    "attachment; filename=\"" + meta.getFilename() + "\"")
            .body(new InputStreamResource(stream));
}
```

`InputStreamResource` wraps the `InputStream` and Spring writes chunks to the HTTP response as the client reads them — **the full file never sits in JVM memory.** The `Content-Disposition: attachment` header tells the browser to download the file rather than displaying it inline.

For truly large files or long-running generation (like CSV exports), `StreamingResponseBody` gives you direct access to the `OutputStream`. This is the pattern for responses that take time to generate — the response starts immediately and data streams as it becomes available, avoiding both memory issues and HTTP timeout problems.

One detail often overlooked: always set `Content-Length` when you know the file size. Without it, Spring uses chunked transfer encoding, which prevents the client from showing a progress bar. For stored files where the size is known, add `.contentLength(meta.getFileSize())` to the response builder.

Spring HATEOAS provides `WebMvcLinkBuilder` to generate links from controller method references — no hardcoded URLs, no string concatenation. Links update automatically when you rename methods or change request mappings.

```java
@GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
public EntityModel<Order> getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
    Order order = orderService.findById(id);
    EntityModel<Order> model = EntityModel.of(order);
    model.add(linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).getOrder(id)).withSelfRel());

    if (order.getStatus() == Status.CREATED) {
        model.add(linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).cancelOrder(id)).withRel("cancel"));
        model.add(linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).payOrder(id)).withRel("pay"));
    }
    if (order.getStatus() == Status.PAID) {
        model.add(linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).shipOrder(id)).withRel("ship"));
    }
    return model;
}
```

The response for a newly created order includes `cancel` and `pay` links. Once paid, those disappear and `ship` appears. The client renders buttons based on which links exist — **no client-side business logic about which actions are valid for which states.** When business rules change (e.g., adding a "hold" state), you update the server and clients adapt automatically.

`RepresentationModelAssembler` extracts this logic into a reusable component when multiple endpoints return the same resource type, preventing link-building duplication across controllers.

A common shortcut is using one DTO for both request and response. This creates a different coupling problem: the fields a client sends to create a resource are rarely the same fields they need in the response.

A `CreateUserRequest` has `name`, `email`, `password`. A `UserResponse` has `id`, `name`, `email`, `createdAt` — no password, and includes server-generated fields the client never sends. Using one class means either accepting fields you shouldn't (`id` in a create request) or annotating half the fields with `@JsonIgnore` depending on direction.

```java
public record CreateUserRequest(
    @NotBlank String name,
    @Email String email,
    @Size(min = 8) String password
) {}

public record UserResponse(
    Long id,
    String name,
    String email,
    LocalDateTime createdAt
) {}
```

Java `record` classes are ideal for DTOs — they're immutable, generate `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString`, and the constructor enforces that all fields are provided. Validation annotations (`@NotBlank`, `@Email`) go on request DTOs only, keeping response classes clean.

For mapping between entities and DTOs, **MapStruct** generates type-safe mapping code at compile time — no reflection, no runtime overhead. A single interface with `@Mapper` produces the implementation automatically. This eliminates the boilerplate objection that makes developers skip DTOs in the first place.

One pattern worth knowing: **projection DTOs** for list endpoints. A `UserListItem` with just `id`, `name`, and `role` is lighter than the full `UserResponse` with all fields. Spring Data JPA even supports interface-based projections that generate the query to select only the needed columns, reducing both network payload and database load.

The conversion between entities and DTOs needs a home. Putting it in the controller clutters request handling. Putting it in the service mixes business logic with serialization concerns. The cleanest approach is a dedicated **mapper layer**.

MapStruct is the standard choice for Java projects. You define a mapper interface, annotate it with `@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")`, and MapStruct generates the implementation at compile time.

```java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface UserMapper {
    UserResponse toResponse(User entity);
    User toEntity(CreateUserRequest request);

    @Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "createdAt", ignore = true)
    void updateEntity(UpdateUserRequest request, @MappingTarget User entity);
}
```

The `@MappingTarget` annotation handles update operations — MapStruct applies only the fields from the request to the existing entity, preserving server-managed fields like `id` and `createdAt`. This eliminates the manual field-by-field copy that's both tedious and error-prone.

The generated code is plain Java method calls — no reflection, no proxy magic, full IDE debuggability. This matters when a mapping bug surfaces in production: you can step through the generated source instead of debugging a runtime framework.
